1
00:00:05,130 --> 00:00:08,213
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is Bhūmīja Sutta.

2
00:00:09,654 --> 00:00:16,960
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the Sanghūtanikāya 12, the Nidāna Sanghūta, number 25.

3
00:00:20,303 --> 00:00:21,343
[SPEAKER_00]: So, add sarvati.

4
00:00:22,704 --> 00:00:29,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, in the evening, the Venerable Bhūmīja, having emerged from seclusion, approached the Venerable Sariputta.

5
00:00:30,432 --> 00:00:33,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Having approached, he exchanged greetings together with the Venerable Sariputta.

6
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:39,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Having exchanged polite greetings of friendliness, the Venerable Bhūmija said this to Venerable Sariputta.

7
00:00:40,995 --> 00:00:51,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Friend Sariputta, there are some ascetics and brāhmins with the doctrine of action who declare that pleasure and pain is made by oneself.

8
00:00:54,559 --> 00:01:08,304
[SPEAKER_00]: And, friend Sariputta, there are some ascetics and brahmins with a doctrine of action who declare that pleasure and pain is made by something else.

9
00:01:11,005 --> 00:01:12,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Something apart from oneself.

10
00:01:13,545 --> 00:01:17,227
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I... Yeah, it's usually...

11
00:01:17,247 --> 00:01:20,548
[SPEAKER_00]: I've seen two of the main translations and it's normally

12
00:01:21,962 --> 00:01:27,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure and pain is made by oneself, or pleasure and pain is made by others.

13
00:01:29,488 --> 00:01:32,190
[SPEAKER_00]: But I wonder if it... perhaps it'd be more accurate.

14
00:01:32,530 --> 00:01:44,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Parasankhatang, parang... sorry, parangkatang, made by para, is not other people, but just anything other than me.

15
00:01:45,219 --> 00:01:45,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

16
00:01:47,161 --> 00:01:48,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's... that's more comprehensive.

17
00:01:50,235 --> 00:02:01,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything other than me includes other people, but also includes other things that are not necessarily people, but can still be the reason for me being pushed into action.

18
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:15,725
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, eh... Yeah, so, the things around you, the, eh...

19
00:02:17,737 --> 00:02:28,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, to a degree, the environment, the accidents, random elements...

20
00:02:28,741 --> 00:02:30,522
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've got...

21
00:02:30,782 --> 00:02:42,906
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but the point there, that needs to be highlighted is, whether the action is done as initiated by you, or initiated by other things apart from you,

22
00:02:44,349 --> 00:02:47,476
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not free from ignorance, you're responsible for your action.

23
00:02:47,637 --> 00:02:47,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

24
00:02:48,378 --> 00:02:48,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

25
00:02:48,639 --> 00:02:51,886
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the the saṃsāra is so perilous.

26
00:02:51,926 --> 00:02:53,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that are not in your control

27
00:02:54,221 --> 00:02:55,242
[SPEAKER_00]: You are responsible for.

28
00:02:55,262 --> 00:02:55,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

29
00:02:56,362 --> 00:03:01,886
[SPEAKER_00]: I think that'll come up again in a bit later, because that's something that seems to get overlooked in the normal translations of this.

30
00:03:02,026 --> 00:03:02,227
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh-huh.

31
00:03:02,247 --> 00:03:02,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

32
00:03:02,427 --> 00:03:02,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Go for it.

33
00:03:02,967 --> 00:03:04,068
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh...

34
00:03:05,169 --> 00:03:14,535
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've got pleasure and pain is made by oneself, pleasure and pain... or there's some ascetics and brahmins who... who declare that pleasure and pain is made by something else.

35
00:03:14,995 --> 00:03:21,480
[SPEAKER_00]: There are some ascetic and brahmins who, with the doctrine of action, who declare that pleasure and pain is made by oneself and made by something else.

36
00:03:22,261 --> 00:03:23,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, friend Sariputta,

37
00:03:36,304 --> 00:03:37,525
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha's one who says what.

38
00:03:37,945 --> 00:03:39,146
[SPEAKER_00]: He's one who declares what.

39
00:03:39,546 --> 00:03:48,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Describing in what way would we be speakers of what was said by the Buddha, so that we could not be accused of describing the Buddha falsely, but in accordance with the dhamma.

40
00:03:48,871 --> 00:03:52,413
[SPEAKER_00]: And no reasonable verbal attack could give ground for criticism.

41
00:03:54,955 --> 00:03:58,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Friend, it was said by the Buddha that pleasure and pain are dependently arisen.

42
00:03:59,757 --> 00:04:00,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of what?

43
00:04:01,298 --> 00:04:03,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of... contact.

44
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:04,260
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

45
00:04:07,228 --> 00:04:09,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you've used the word pressure.

46
00:04:10,531 --> 00:04:10,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

47
00:04:13,373 --> 00:04:25,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Speaking thus, one would be a speaker of what was said by the Buddha, and one could not be accused of describing the Buddha falsely, but in accordance with the dhamma, and no reasonable verbal attack could give ground for criticism.

48
00:04:28,064 --> 00:04:34,770
[SPEAKER_00]: In the case, friend, of those ascetics and brahmins with a doctrine of action, who declare that pleasure and pain is made by oneself,

49
00:04:35,908 --> 00:04:38,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that is with the support of pressure.

50
00:04:40,290 --> 00:04:46,913
[SPEAKER_00]: In the case, friend, of those ascetics and brahmins with adoption have actually declared that pleasure and pain are made by something else, made by oneself and made by something else.

51
00:04:47,354 --> 00:04:50,815
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not made by oneself and not made by something else.

52
00:04:50,835 --> 00:04:52,056
[SPEAKER_00]: That is determined by the pressure.

53
00:04:52,216 --> 00:04:52,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

54
00:04:54,437 --> 00:04:59,900
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, their view is secondary, yet it presents as a representative of what's first.

55
00:05:01,371 --> 00:05:07,034
[SPEAKER_01]: They say the action is because of this, but they fail to see that they arrived at the conclusion because they're already acting out of the pressure.

56
00:05:10,335 --> 00:05:11,436
[SPEAKER_00]: They're trying to explain the same thing.

57
00:05:11,456 --> 00:05:12,597
[SPEAKER_00]: They're trying to explain the pressure.

58
00:05:12,697 --> 00:05:14,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is responsible for that.

59
00:05:14,518 --> 00:05:19,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Which in itself then implies that, look, this is how things are, although it's a result of the pressure.

60
00:05:21,001 --> 00:05:24,783
[SPEAKER_01]: My explanation of the pressure is the reason for the pressure, pretty much.

61
00:05:27,144 --> 00:05:29,365
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, that's in its nature, every wrong view.

62
00:05:30,605 --> 00:05:31,306
[SPEAKER_01]: When it's secondary.

63
00:05:33,307 --> 00:05:41,614
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the only way to deal with the wrong view is to see the dependent, simultaneous presence of two things, or patitsaṁpāda.

64
00:05:42,795 --> 00:05:44,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't explain it.

65
00:05:45,278 --> 00:05:47,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, you can explain it, but then it's not patitsaṁpāda.

66
00:05:47,479 --> 00:05:48,600
[SPEAKER_01]: You can either see directly.

67
00:05:49,301 --> 00:05:52,083
[SPEAKER_01]: If it falls on the level of explanation, it means it's not seen directly.

68
00:05:53,124 --> 00:05:53,885
[SPEAKER_01]: You're explaining it.

69
00:05:55,026 --> 00:05:58,129
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why every patitsaṁpāda starts with the principle with this, this is.

70
00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:01,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Not after this, this comes to be.

71
00:06:02,242 --> 00:06:02,382
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

72
00:06:02,662 --> 00:06:04,343
[SPEAKER_01]: With the presence of this, this is present.

73
00:06:05,083 --> 00:06:08,805
[SPEAKER_01]: So, with the presence of pressure, acting out of being pressured is present.

74
00:06:11,567 --> 00:06:18,351
[SPEAKER_01]: And if your action out of being pressured is the explanation of how the pressure came to be, it's going to be incorrect.

75
00:06:19,887 --> 00:06:29,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were truly unaffected by that pressure, which is a position of being able to understand it and not act out of it, you wouldn't be pressured in the first place, you wouldn't be acting out of it.

76
00:06:29,770 --> 00:06:31,330
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you would be describing that.

77
00:06:34,651 --> 00:06:36,891
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can see that even, like, on a practical basis.

78
00:06:38,692 --> 00:06:42,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even intentions, in a way, are a form of a pressure.

79
00:06:44,494 --> 00:06:45,874
[SPEAKER_01]: They're bound, they're inseparable.

80
00:06:46,134 --> 00:06:53,096
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have intention to do something by body, speech or mind if there is no simultaneous presence of some form of pressure.

81
00:07:02,439 --> 00:07:03,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if your intentions are good,

82
00:07:05,372 --> 00:07:08,213
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're still acting out of pressure, you're still acting out of ignorance.

83
00:07:08,753 --> 00:07:12,134
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why no amount of good action will result in freedom from ignorance.

84
00:07:12,454 --> 00:07:17,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Arahant is free from good and bad.

85
00:07:19,397 --> 00:07:23,418
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the only way to start dealing with the pressure is to start taking responsibility for your intentions.

86
00:07:24,118 --> 00:07:26,919
[SPEAKER_01]: And, of course, actions, and then take responsibility for intentions.

87
00:07:27,740 --> 00:07:32,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you get to see that you're actually acting out of pressure, and that's what's wrong in itself.

88
00:07:33,362 --> 00:07:34,362
[SPEAKER_00]: So that... so...

89
00:07:38,784 --> 00:07:48,891
[SPEAKER_00]: So, taking responsibility for one's actions, but recognizing that that is... there simultaneously with... With the pressure.

90
00:07:48,991 --> 00:07:49,411
[SPEAKER_00]: The pressure.

91
00:07:49,811 --> 00:07:49,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

92
00:07:50,011 --> 00:07:51,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is always already there.

93
00:07:51,112 --> 00:08:04,981
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not... By taking responsibility for your intentions, that are there simultaneously with the pressure, and which is how you basically end up allowing the pressure to manifest, by acting out of it, you get to see that your...

94
00:08:06,327 --> 00:08:08,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Your responsibility, that's where the line is.

95
00:08:08,573 --> 00:08:09,916
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your intentions and your actions.

96
00:08:10,437 --> 00:08:15,470
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not responsible for the pressure being there, but you're responsible for being pressured.

97
00:08:16,517 --> 00:08:17,357
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a different thing.

98
00:08:17,597 --> 00:08:21,578
[SPEAKER_01]: That's something that you induce and maintain through acting out of pressure.

99
00:08:22,018 --> 00:08:24,879
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you give the pressure, the power over you to pressure you.

100
00:08:25,319 --> 00:08:26,679
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Buddha said that.

101
00:08:27,159 --> 00:08:31,440
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because the monks go and take what belongs to Mara, not to them.

102
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:32,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Mara does with them what he wants.

103
00:08:32,901 --> 00:08:33,901
[SPEAKER_01]: So you let him in.

104
00:08:34,301 --> 00:08:37,582
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't come in, unless you open the door and pull him by the hand.

105
00:08:37,662 --> 00:08:37,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Come in.

106
00:08:39,302 --> 00:08:40,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Or go and take his bait.

107
00:08:42,083 --> 00:08:44,043
[SPEAKER_01]: But not knowing that that's where the line is,

108
00:08:44,799 --> 00:08:49,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you would be trying to deny the pressure, or get rid of the pressure, because of that pressure.

109
00:08:50,323 --> 00:08:56,347
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, your practice becomes practice of denying the arisen phenomena, because you're affected by the same arisen phenomena in the first place.

110
00:08:57,228 --> 00:08:58,749
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the practice of frustration.

111
00:09:04,172 --> 00:09:13,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, go the other way, of don't take responsibility for the pressure, because it's there on its own, and then, avoid responsibility for your intentions and actions that come out of it, because they're there on their own as well.

112
00:09:15,272 --> 00:09:15,832
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are not.

113
00:09:17,153 --> 00:09:21,457
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you need to find the middle way, as in where the line is, where your responsibility is, and then not abandon it.

114
00:09:22,558 --> 00:09:39,391
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha would say, whenever there is a... like an unwholesome thought would arise in his mind, he would not welcome it, not delight in it, not entertain it, not act out of it, and get rid of it, basically, by not feeding it to stay longer.

115
00:09:40,172 --> 00:09:42,734
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't say he would prevent it from arising in the first place.

116
00:09:42,994 --> 00:09:43,154
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

117
00:09:44,357 --> 00:09:54,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the Buddha had the mārā, the thoughts of mārā coming to him, certainly for the first few years, and then eventually mārā gave up, because he realized there's absolutely no entry.

118
00:09:55,300 --> 00:10:01,941
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as there is a possibility of entry, he'll keep trying to get in, as in there will be a pressure manifested to an extent.

119
00:10:01,981 --> 00:10:05,742
[SPEAKER_01]: But whether you're pressured by it or not, that's a completely different thing.

120
00:10:08,482 --> 00:10:10,543
[SPEAKER_01]: So equating pressure with being pressured,

121
00:10:11,297 --> 00:10:14,578
[SPEAKER_01]: That means, basically, acting automatically out of it.

122
00:10:18,139 --> 00:10:22,360
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you stop... You don't see the possibility of there being pressure, but not acting out.

123
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:22,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

124
00:10:23,460 --> 00:10:26,281
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where you, like, either overshoot or undershoot.

125
00:10:26,381 --> 00:10:27,541
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't see the middle ground.

126
00:10:33,203 --> 00:10:34,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

127
00:10:37,644 --> 00:10:38,864
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, in this case, friend,

128
00:10:40,247 --> 00:10:50,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Of those ascetics and brahmins with the doctrine of action who declare that pleasure and pain is made by oneself, that they will feel without pressure, that is not possible.

129
00:10:51,518 --> 00:11:05,031
[SPEAKER_00]: In the case, friends, of those ascetics and brahmins who declare pleasure and pain is made by something else, by both, by neither, that they will feel without pressure, that is not possible.

130
00:11:08,487 --> 00:11:13,388
[SPEAKER_00]: The Venerable Ānanda heard the Venerable Sariputta's conversation with the Venerable Bhūmijā.

131
00:11:14,068 --> 00:11:15,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the Venerable Ānanda approached the Buddha.

132
00:11:16,588 --> 00:11:19,589
[SPEAKER_00]: Having approached, having paid respect to the Buddha, he sat down at one side.

133
00:11:20,289 --> 00:11:26,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Sitting down at one side, the Venerable Ānanda told the Buddha about the entire conversation between Sariputta and Bhūmijā.

134
00:11:28,650 --> 00:11:29,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Good, good, Ānanda.

135
00:11:30,171 --> 00:11:32,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Answering rightly, one would answer like this, Sariputta.

136
00:11:34,091 --> 00:11:37,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Ānanda, it was said by me that pleasure and pain are dependently arisen.

137
00:11:39,213 --> 00:11:39,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of what?

138
00:11:39,834 --> 00:11:40,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of pressure.

139
00:11:41,675 --> 00:11:44,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Speaking thus, one would be a speaker of what's said by me.

140
00:11:46,720 --> 00:11:49,402
[SPEAKER_00]: No reasonable verbal attack would give ground for criticism.

141
00:11:50,183 --> 00:11:59,171
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you can ask there, see, because it says, pressure, presence of pressure, depending on the arising, presence of pressure means presence of pleasure and pain.

142
00:12:01,454 --> 00:12:01,674
[SPEAKER_01]: But,

143
00:12:02,425 --> 00:12:07,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, you're either putujana or an ārya that results in ārahanca.

144
00:12:07,328 --> 00:12:10,949
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing... there is no third option for a human being to be.

145
00:12:12,050 --> 00:12:15,932
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're either with the grain of ignorance or you're against the grain of ignorance.

146
00:12:17,833 --> 00:12:24,196
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, there is your responsibility in that pressure, not for the presence of the pressure, but for ignoring the pressure.

147
00:12:25,677 --> 00:12:27,858
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for putujana, pressure

148
00:12:28,752 --> 00:12:32,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the simultaneously present cause for feeling pain and feeling pleasure?

149
00:12:33,116 --> 00:12:35,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he's ignorant of the pressure.

150
00:12:36,959 --> 00:12:43,984
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, his doing is on the level of his own ignorance of the pressure, not on the level of that which pressured him.

151
00:12:44,525 --> 00:12:45,505
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just a bait.

152
00:12:48,167 --> 00:12:51,070
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have no say over the bait, because that's what Mara's domain is.

153
00:12:52,845 --> 00:12:55,146
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what people don't understand when it comes to paticampāna.

154
00:12:55,166 --> 00:12:57,447
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't understand it theoretically.

155
00:12:57,467 --> 00:12:59,427
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either with it or against it.

156
00:12:59,587 --> 00:13:03,109
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either with the grain or against the grain, which means you either don't understand it or you understand it.

157
00:13:04,029 --> 00:13:07,790
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have an accurate representation of it in theory and still be a bhutujana.

158
00:13:08,410 --> 00:13:10,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you understand it, you're a sotāpanna.

159
00:13:10,391 --> 00:13:16,613
[SPEAKER_01]: If you understand that pleasure and pain are independently arisen upon the pressure that's being ignored, that's being not understood.

160
00:13:18,734 --> 00:13:21,515
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand it by seeing it as such.

161
00:13:23,712 --> 00:13:34,396
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as people don't see that that's the only... those are the only possible situations you can either be affected by it or not, they will then use paticcaṁpāda and all this as a form of explanation.

162
00:13:34,716 --> 00:13:36,957
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, see, pleasure and pain arise because of contact.

163
00:13:36,977 --> 00:13:39,758
[SPEAKER_01]: And contact is this mechanical presence of the eye which... No, it's not.

164
00:13:40,658 --> 00:13:41,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Contact is the pleasure.

165
00:13:41,799 --> 00:13:42,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Or a kind of technique.

166
00:13:43,379 --> 00:13:47,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because you don't need to be ignorant to have your eyes looking, don't you?

167
00:13:47,681 --> 00:13:49,802
[SPEAKER_01]: So arahant has eyes... has sights, doesn't he?

168
00:13:51,499 --> 00:14:01,568
[SPEAKER_01]: But if truly that's like based on some sort of mechanical setup of paticca-upāda, means uprooting ignorance, would then uproot all the senses.

169
00:14:01,608 --> 00:14:02,528
[SPEAKER_01]: They wouldn't be there.

170
00:14:03,589 --> 00:14:04,110
[SPEAKER_01]: But it isn't.

171
00:14:05,071 --> 00:14:11,956
[SPEAKER_01]: So the pressure is the cause for pleasure and pain, when ignorance is the cause for the pressure.

172
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:16,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas the other way?

173
00:14:17,857 --> 00:14:20,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of ignorance means absence of you being pressured.

174
00:14:20,799 --> 00:14:24,322
[SPEAKER_01]: You being pressured means you won't feel anything on account of the pressure.

175
00:14:25,303 --> 00:14:25,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Good or bad?

176
00:14:26,083 --> 00:14:27,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasant or unpleasant?

177
00:14:27,384 --> 00:14:28,645
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be beyond the feeling.

178
00:14:28,825 --> 00:14:29,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

179
00:14:33,388 --> 00:14:37,932
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't be understanding the pressure correctly while you're being pressured by it.

180
00:14:38,432 --> 00:14:38,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

181
00:14:38,932 --> 00:14:43,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you cannot be certainly conveying the pressure correctly while you're pressured by it.

182
00:14:43,436 --> 00:14:45,557
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what it says in the beginning of that sutta.

183
00:14:46,078 --> 00:14:48,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever statement they make, it's because they're pressured.

184
00:14:48,980 --> 00:14:50,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why that statement is wrong.

185
00:14:50,321 --> 00:14:50,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

186
00:14:52,262 --> 00:14:55,665
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can only understand it when you aren't being pressured.

187
00:14:56,005 --> 00:14:56,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

188
00:14:56,666 --> 00:14:59,068
[SPEAKER_01]: And you won't be pressured by it when you understand it.

189
00:14:59,448 --> 00:14:59,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

190
00:15:02,790 --> 00:15:06,113
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, I mean, how many times the Buddha would say... Yeah.

191
00:15:06,636 --> 00:15:08,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowledge has arisen, understanding has arisen.

192
00:15:09,157 --> 00:15:15,161
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm free from, basically, pressure and pain and any suffering, based on the understanding.

193
00:15:15,201 --> 00:15:17,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Not based on a theoretical explanation that pleases my view.

194
00:15:27,648 --> 00:15:28,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there more to sutta?

195
00:15:28,848 --> 00:15:33,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there's this big... this important bit, which, eh...

196
00:15:34,252 --> 00:15:34,872
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't think it's...

197
00:15:36,863 --> 00:15:41,686
[SPEAKER_00]: I've talked about it enough, because I think people just don't understand it, so they just ignore it.

198
00:15:47,830 --> 00:15:48,830
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just go forward to that.

199
00:15:48,930 --> 00:16:04,240
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he says... For Ananda, when there is body, on account of bodily intention, pleasure and pain arise internally.

200
00:16:05,134 --> 00:16:10,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, Ananda, when there is speech on account of verbal intention, pleasure and pain arise internally.

201
00:16:10,798 --> 00:16:15,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, Ananda, when there is mind on account of mental intention, pleasure and pain arise internally.

202
00:16:16,602 --> 00:16:18,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And with the support of ignorance.

203
00:16:18,643 --> 00:16:18,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Hmm.

204
00:16:20,344 --> 00:16:29,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Ananda, either he himself intends that bodily intention because of which pleasure and pain arises internally.

205
00:16:30,698 --> 00:16:39,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, Ananda, things other than him intend that bodily intention because of which pleasure and pain arises internally.

206
00:16:39,423 --> 00:16:41,844
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what does that say?

207
00:16:41,984 --> 00:16:47,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's intended by you or by anything else, it's because you're pressured in the first place.

208
00:16:50,329 --> 00:16:57,613
[SPEAKER_00]: And the thing that the usual translations of this seem to be saying is that

209
00:17:01,823 --> 00:17:04,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Pick another point you said earlier.

210
00:17:04,186 --> 00:17:15,456
[SPEAKER_00]: The usual translation is either one intends a bodily intention for oneself or out of one's own initiative.

211
00:17:17,853 --> 00:17:27,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, other people instigate, or other people encourage you to... to... to intend that bodily intention.

212
00:17:27,506 --> 00:17:27,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

213
00:17:27,867 --> 00:17:30,911
[SPEAKER_00]: In both cases, you are doing the intending.

214
00:17:30,951 --> 00:17:31,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the point.

215
00:17:31,732 --> 00:17:33,794
[SPEAKER_01]: It's me, or anything else apart from me.

216
00:17:33,915 --> 00:17:34,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

217
00:17:34,415 --> 00:17:36,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, it's done because of the ignorance of the pressure.

218
00:17:37,356 --> 00:17:37,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

219
00:17:37,636 --> 00:17:40,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, I'm responsible for it.

220
00:17:40,297 --> 00:17:41,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, I've done this because of this or that.

221
00:17:41,978 --> 00:17:45,399
[SPEAKER_01]: You've done that because you're responsible for what you chose to do.

222
00:17:45,579 --> 00:17:47,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if the other thing sort of did it for you.

223
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:48,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

224
00:17:48,720 --> 00:17:52,222
[SPEAKER_01]: You were responsible for being affected by the other thing that resulted in this action.

225
00:17:56,575 --> 00:17:57,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's exactly what it says.

226
00:17:58,476 --> 00:18:04,197
[SPEAKER_01]: What is done by himself or by anything apart from himself, it's when there is ignorance in regard to it.

227
00:18:05,498 --> 00:18:09,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

228
00:18:09,699 --> 00:18:09,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

229
00:18:09,919 --> 00:18:14,800
[SPEAKER_01]: See, as we just said five minutes ago, if there is no ignorance in regard to pressure, you won't be pressured.

230
00:18:15,521 --> 00:18:19,702
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not pressured, no action will be rooted in the pressure, internally or externally.

231
00:18:30,820 --> 00:18:44,410
[SPEAKER_00]: So, either oneself, either he himself intends that bodily intention because of which pleasure and pain arises internally, or things other than him intend that bodily intention because of which pleasure and pain arises internally.

232
00:18:45,210 --> 00:18:51,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, things that ordinarily you would think, well, if it's not me, it's something else, then that's not my responsibility.

233
00:18:53,895 --> 00:18:54,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

234
00:18:54,295 --> 00:18:54,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

235
00:18:54,555 --> 00:18:54,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

236
00:18:54,875 --> 00:18:56,596
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what people think.

237
00:18:56,616 --> 00:18:56,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

238
00:18:56,836 --> 00:18:57,937
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the whole point.

239
00:18:57,957 --> 00:19:00,078
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether it's done internally or externally,

240
00:19:15,415 --> 00:19:17,656
[SPEAKER_01]: It's done on the basis of the pressure.

241
00:19:17,737 --> 00:19:18,477
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what matters.

242
00:19:18,938 --> 00:19:21,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's done internally with awareness or without awareness.

243
00:19:21,620 --> 00:19:23,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's done externally with awareness or without.

244
00:19:23,121 --> 00:19:23,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's done with both.

245
00:19:23,882 --> 00:19:24,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's done with neither.

246
00:19:25,403 --> 00:19:29,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Any possible permutation of doing it is secondary to the reason why you're doing it.

247
00:19:37,928 --> 00:19:40,610
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for putujana, you can just say pressure.

248
00:19:41,370 --> 00:19:48,955
[SPEAKER_01]: But the problem is, for putujana, they will say, okay, so this pressure that has arisen on account of, whatever, physical things, that's why these acts happen.

249
00:19:49,055 --> 00:19:49,195
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

250
00:19:50,216 --> 00:19:56,020
[SPEAKER_01]: The pressure that has arisen on account of physical things does not automatically mean you need to be emotionally pressured by it.

251
00:19:56,100 --> 00:19:57,801
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your ignorance of that pressure.

252
00:20:00,082 --> 00:20:03,965
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't matter why, how, to what extent, to what permutation actions are done,

253
00:20:04,989 --> 00:20:10,294
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's rooted in you feeling on account of being pressured, that's the problem.

254
00:20:10,414 --> 00:20:12,876
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the issue is and the freedom from it is.

255
00:20:18,221 --> 00:20:18,962
[SPEAKER_00]: So then it repeats.

256
00:20:19,042 --> 00:20:21,184
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what we keep saying in all the other talks as well.

257
00:20:22,005 --> 00:20:23,987
[SPEAKER_01]: The difference between the management of suffering

258
00:20:24,797 --> 00:20:34,228
[SPEAKER_01]: As in doing things on account of it, in order to prevent it and deal... And the difference between that and the difference between not being affected by it in the first place, whereby you don't have to do anything.

259
00:20:34,789 --> 00:20:36,911
[SPEAKER_01]: No burden, no duty, no requirement.

260
00:20:37,032 --> 00:20:39,354
[SPEAKER_01]: You're free in the beginning, the middle and in the end.

261
00:20:41,136 --> 00:20:43,940
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when you have removed the ignorance in regard to the persisting pressure.

262
00:20:56,313 --> 00:21:18,885
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, repeats Ananda, either he himself intends that mental, then verbal and mental intention, or things other than him intends, or either with awareness, verbal, mental, or without awareness, he intends that mental intention because of which passion, pain arises.

263
00:21:18,965 --> 00:21:21,607
[SPEAKER_01]: So, well, that also says that fundamentally,

264
00:21:23,597 --> 00:21:26,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Puthujjana's intentions cannot be his own either.

265
00:21:27,598 --> 00:21:27,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

266
00:21:28,079 --> 00:21:31,680
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, Puthujjana cannot own even his own will.

267
00:21:33,220 --> 00:21:34,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Structurally speaking.

268
00:21:34,721 --> 00:21:36,701
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where possibility of arahantship is possible.

269
00:21:37,662 --> 00:21:38,262
[SPEAKER_01]: It's manifest.

270
00:21:38,602 --> 00:21:41,283
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you can become, like, free from any appropriation.

271
00:21:44,590 --> 00:21:51,353
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as there is ignorance in regard to the pressure that makes him feel, that would be the reason for his action.

272
00:21:51,414 --> 00:21:57,056
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, that ignorance is gratuitous, because intentions would be operating on their own, as they do in an arahant.

273
00:21:57,537 --> 00:22:00,498
[SPEAKER_01]: But as the Buddha said, although gratuitous, ignorance is beginningless.

274
00:22:01,519 --> 00:22:05,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Although it's a parasite, it shouldn't be there, it's beginningless as such.

275
00:22:06,121 --> 00:22:11,604
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why once you remove it, it cannot come back, because it was never there to begin with in the same sense.

276
00:22:12,144 --> 00:22:13,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that it can never be there,

277
00:22:14,323 --> 00:22:15,083
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it was there.

278
00:22:16,344 --> 00:22:24,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the knowledge of not knowing that ignorance cannot penetrate the intention and aggregates, is what makes you ignorant of the aggregates.

279
00:22:25,389 --> 00:22:30,012
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it was all said upādāna is not the five aggregates, but it's not found anywhere apart from it.

280
00:22:31,572 --> 00:22:33,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance in regard to the five aggregates, upādāna is there.

281
00:22:34,454 --> 00:22:38,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire and lust in regard to the five aggregates, upādāna is there.

282
00:22:38,316 --> 00:22:39,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Removal of those, removal of upādāna.

283
00:22:40,217 --> 00:22:41,438
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot reintroduce it then.

284
00:22:47,857 --> 00:23:00,583
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way that Kutujana will be able to overcome actions that come out of intentions caused by him, or everything else apart from him, is to start taking responsibility for how he feels.

285
00:23:01,681 --> 00:23:03,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And how he feels is how he's pressured.

286
00:23:04,102 --> 00:23:06,623
[SPEAKER_01]: And how he's pressured is how much he's ignorant of the pressure.

287
00:23:06,783 --> 00:23:08,803
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not what you choose, basically.

288
00:23:08,823 --> 00:23:09,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

289
00:23:09,184 --> 00:23:10,244
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a matter of choice.

290
00:23:10,544 --> 00:23:12,265
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a matter of...

291
00:23:12,385 --> 00:23:17,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice is already secondary to you being pressured, which is why Arahant still has a choice.

292
00:23:17,446 --> 00:23:24,329
[SPEAKER_01]: If choice were the true reason for the ignorance and the pressure, it would disappear in an Arahant.

293
00:23:24,469 --> 00:23:25,049
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't.

294
00:23:29,788 --> 00:23:33,811
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the choice, sure, can maintain your state of ignorance.

295
00:23:33,851 --> 00:23:39,715
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, more ignoring will result in your ignorant choices, choices of sensuality, ill-will, distraction.

296
00:23:39,856 --> 00:23:40,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

297
00:23:40,856 --> 00:23:42,257
[SPEAKER_01]: But even in that sense, it's secondary.

298
00:23:42,277 --> 00:23:47,461
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the sense restraint is necessary as a basis for practice, but it's not directly related to dhamma.

299
00:23:47,541 --> 00:23:49,603
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't automatically result in arahantship.

300
00:23:51,104 --> 00:23:53,246
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no arahantship without a sense restraint.

301
00:23:53,806 --> 00:23:58,810
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no arahantship without restraining your choice, but you can't become an arahant by choice.

302
00:24:01,965 --> 00:24:08,110
[SPEAKER_01]: It's by uprooting that which... That which, basically, choice is rooted in.

303
00:24:08,130 --> 00:24:09,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly, yeah.

304
00:24:17,297 --> 00:24:19,439
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sutta finishes with...

305
00:24:21,320 --> 00:24:30,708
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's talked about these various ways in which intentions are made.

306
00:24:32,567 --> 00:24:34,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of which, pleasure and pain arises internally.

307
00:24:35,248 --> 00:24:38,649
[SPEAKER_00]: And it says, Ananda, in these things, ignorance is involved.

308
00:24:38,669 --> 00:24:38,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Hmm.

309
00:24:42,591 --> 00:24:46,052
[SPEAKER_00]: With the remainderless fading away and cessation of this very ignorance,

310
00:24:47,262 --> 00:24:52,143
[SPEAKER_00]: There isn't the body because of which that pleasure and pain arises internally.

311
00:24:52,964 --> 00:24:56,365
[SPEAKER_00]: There isn't the speech because of which that pleasure and pain arises internally.

312
00:24:56,865 --> 00:25:00,086
[SPEAKER_00]: There isn't the mind because of which that pleasure and pain arises internally.

313
00:25:00,466 --> 00:25:01,566
[SPEAKER_00]: That field isn't there.

314
00:25:01,986 --> 00:25:03,166
[SPEAKER_00]: That ground isn't there.

315
00:25:03,286 --> 00:25:04,407
[SPEAKER_00]: That domain isn't there.

316
00:25:04,527 --> 00:25:09,708
[SPEAKER_00]: That cause because of which that pleasure and pain arises internally isn't there.

317
00:25:10,168 --> 00:25:12,589
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no action out of being pressured.

318
00:25:13,978 --> 00:25:15,099
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're not pressured anymore.

319
00:25:15,899 --> 00:25:18,621
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you act out of pressure, when there is no pressure?

320
00:25:19,141 --> 00:25:26,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you go through the motion of those actions, that's still not action out of... the domain of acting out of pressure has ceased.

321
00:25:27,846 --> 00:25:28,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Irreversibly ceased.

322
00:25:33,529 --> 00:25:36,151
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why arahant can still act and do things.

323
00:25:37,282 --> 00:25:40,125
[SPEAKER_01]: But not out of being contacted in the first place.

324
00:25:40,926 --> 00:25:46,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from Putujan's point of view, who can only act out of pressure, yeah, there is no action in an arahant.

325
00:25:48,936 --> 00:25:52,860
[SPEAKER_01]: But going through the motions of those which used to be pressured actions, yeah, they're still there.

326
00:25:54,903 --> 00:25:56,885
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why an arahant can move, talk and think.

327
00:26:20,388 --> 00:26:20,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, cool.

328
00:26:20,816 --> 00:26:21,162
[SPEAKER_01]: That was...

